A||||19||Doni Hagan||I Wanna Recharge (AC Question)!!||||5||
Z||000000||Doni Hagan||06-29-2001||05:32 PM||||It's my turn to pick some brains. I wanna recharge my AC with an aftermarket  conversion kit. The system is holding pressure but blows warm air (or at least ambiant temp air.) Here's the question.. Should I completely evacuate the system before putting in the refridgerant replacement or should I put it in on top of the other stuff that's in there? While we're on the subject, has anyone used one of these kits and how much of a pain is it? I've been told that if there are no leaks and the system is holding pressure, then it's just a matter of shooting in new coolant.   <P>I'm anticpating getting hammered about my lack of AC knowledge but I'm ready for ya. <P>Thanks. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||maryjane||06-29-2001||06:21 PM||||The 2 134 conversion kits I've used worked fine, got them at autozone. I've never tried putting 134 in on top of r12 if that's what you have in mind. The instructions on the kits tell you to remove all the old freon and as much of the old oil as possible, before recharging with 134. There have been a lot of posts here the last month on this subject,some in detail. try a search. If you have never done any a/c work, read up on it, it can be dangerous, especially working on the hp side. I always pull a vac, it will tell you what shape your connections and seals are in. If it won't hold a vac, it won't hold freon for long.$$$$<BR>Be careful<BR>Don||||reg||1
Z||000002||Songman||06-29-2001||06:27 PM||||DO NOT put R134 in on top of R12. Evacuate the system.<P>Search and read the recent topics on this, and go to Ogre's cave to read his thoughts on it as well...<P>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||maryjane||06-30-2001||12:20 AM||||While I'm thinkin about it, does anybody know if r-12 or r-134 can create phosgene gas like r-11 can? If r-11 comes in contact with a heat source and moisture(sounds absurd) phosgene gas can be created. It is a deadly gas,toxic to most body tissues. There have been instances where r-11 was inhaled thru a burning cigarette & extensive dammage done to the throat and lungs. I believe 5ppm is fatal, but don't quote me on that, been to long ago.<BR>Don ||||reg||4
Z||000004||theogre||06-30-2001||01:46 PM||||R12 and R134 Can Not live in the same system. Preferably all R12 is gone when R134 goes in. If more than 1-2% R12 remains then the head pressures can blow the system apart or fry the compressor.<P>Using R11 is illegal, It was used to flush R12 systems. It cannot be used to flush systems for R134. There are specific flushed for R134.<P>I've not heard of R12 or R134 doing what you aska about. Best place to ask a question like that would be aircondition.com.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Vader||06-30-2001||05:20 PM||||MJ,<P>Both TCM and TCE can create phosgene when exposed to flame or elevated temperatures.<P>(You would probably crap on yourself if you knew what we USED to do with TCE.)<P>Dani,<P>Don't forget to flush and refill with PAO oil or your R134a system will suck even more than a "properly" convverted system.  If you really want respectable performance, use MP-39 as a replacement.<P>------------------<BR>"<I>Let the bodies hit the floor!</I>"||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mach10||06-30-2001||08:50 PM||||Wasn't that stuff more commonly refered to as "mustard gas" in WWI?<BR>Nasty stuff. Is there any viable way to flush an AC system in the garage? Mine has nothing left in it, blown seal, and needs the 134 conversion. I'm confident that replacing the seals will cure my non-AC problem. Thoughts?||||reg||13
Z||000007||Vader||06-30-2001||09:08 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>Wasn't that stuff more commonly refered to as "mustard gas" in WWI?<BR>Nasty stuff. Is there any viable way to flush an AC system in the garage? Mine has nothing left in it, blown seal, and needs the 134 conversion. I'm confident that replacing the seals will cure my non-AC problem. Thoughts?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes, that was called "mustard gas" in "the Big One".<P>Replace the leaking seals, flush the system with a commercially available solvent, then evacuate it.  Once you're holding vacuum, add the correct amount of POE or PAO oil and load with R134a.  <P>If you want the easy and more effective way out (with far better cooling) check into R414a instead of R134a.  It won't overload your compressor like R134a and has a much better latent heat capacity.  R134a (in my opinion) is not worth the trouble, since you'll never have the cooling erffectiveness of the original R12.  R414a actually cools better than R12 at most pressures, and is a helluva lot cheaper.  No PAG/PAO/POE oil conversion either, since it mixes with and carries all type of oil without precipitating solids or gelling.<P>If the seal was blown, at least he was happy.  I'll bet the walrus was jealous, though.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>"<I>Let the bodies hit the floor!</I>"<p>[This message has been edited by Vader (edited 06-30-2001).]||||reg||2
Z||000008||Mach10||07-01-2001||07:48 PM||||How readily available is R414a? What is it's more common application?<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000009||maryjane||07-01-2001||07:55 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>How readily available is R414a? What is it's more common application?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Bump- Mach10's question<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||07-01-2001||11:19 PM||||You might be able to find out a bit more about it on <A HREF="http://www.epa.gov" TARGET=_blank>www.epa.gov</A>  there is a whole list of refrigerants that are aproved. <A HREF="http://www.epa.gov/ozone/title6/609/choice.html" TARGET=_blank>The USEPA, CFC's and Cars.</A><P>Keep in mind that types not on the EPA list are probably illegal to use. Use/Possetion/sale of CFCs by unlicensed persons now carries very large fines and even jail time. Shops selling to unlicensed persons can and have been put out of business by the feds. (Businesses get even bigger fines....)<P>FRom the epa's snap program....<BR>Many refrigerants, including R-401A (made by DuPont), R-401B (DuPont), R-409A (Elf Atochem), Care 30 (Calor Gas), Adak-29/Adak-12 (TACIP Int'l), MT-31 (Millenia Tech), and ES-12R (Intervest), have not been submitted for review in motor vehicle air conditioning, and it is therefore illegal to use these refrigerants in such systems as an alternative to CFC-12.<P>Also, you'll have problems if you need profesional work done. Most shops won't have that and won't work on it. They often won't work on blends of diferant products either. Blends contaminate their very expensive equipment. (allot of shops own refrigerant identifiers. Many newer recovery units have identifiers built in to protect the machine.)<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Mach10||07-02-2001||03:42 AM||||Well, according to the EPA, R-414A and R-414B are both acceptable R-12 substitutes. Now, as to how readily available they are, that remains to be seen. I'll go talk to my Grease Monkey, as I'm ill-equipped and leary of dealing with AC.||||reg||6
Z||000012||BC-GT||07-02-2001||01:30 PM||||Try this for a good substitute <A HREF="http://www.refrigerantsales.com/autofrost.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.refrigerantsales.com/autofrost.html</A> <P>------------------<BR>Darryl Martin<BR>1985 GT <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/pf251a969c96964ef15691e97e7ac4489/feb64e3a.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||cadaver||07-04-2001||02:29 AM||||I found that R12 works just fine. Why is everyone converting?||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||07-04-2001||12:40 PM||||In my case,,, because recharging R12 legally would cost $200-300 dollars. Just the Freon is $40+ a pound now at most shops.<P>I had nothing in my system to start with. I can change the acc/dryer for $65-75 after fixing a hose loose connecion. Then charge the R134 for less than $50.<P>I had the thing blowing ice cubes out until I found another loose joint hidden in a corner... Man this thing is a plumber's nightmare to get to everything. I thought I had all the joints found before charging but there is one hidden in a corner. had to hand trace the lines every inch before I found it.<P>The thing I #1 don't like about the kits is that everyone just charges based on labeled system volume. Bad idea. You don't know what the real system volume is because you didn't get the old oil out. <P>The only way I'll charge by volume is with a totally clean system. In all cases you must know high and low side pressure. Under charging you just have lousy operation but Over charging can blow things apart or fry the compressor. R12 you had a charge tolerance of about 1/2 a pound. R134 has a tolerance of about +-2 ounces.||||reg||1
Z||000015||MontanaFiero||07-05-2001||03:09 PM||||Many "acceptable" R-12 substitutes are not compatible with the R-12 system. Many of them will not dissolve in the oil, so the compressor will not stay lubricated-- thus compressor failure. <B>The only <I>acceptable</I> solution I have found is AutoFrost (R406A)</B>, which will provide adequate lubrication, and does not require an oil change or flush, like 134A does. REMEMBER, <I>always</I> replace the Dryer (the silver canister in the front), the orifice tube, and the seals--seals need to be neoprene.  Always pull a vacuum (at a shop) before adding any refrigerant of any kind. NEVER mix refrigerants. <P>AUTOFROST-- <A HREF="http://www.autofrost.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.autofrost.com</A> <BR>all data sheets are available there.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/blee291/fiero/mtfieroplate.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by MontanaFiero (edited 07-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||cadaver||07-07-2001||06:13 PM||||Even at $40 a pound thats only $100 in freon. 2 1/2 pounds. And R-12 is not that hard to find. And if you buy it yourself its not that expensive either. And the way to find leaks is not with a vacuum. Yes you do need to evacuate the system but when the system is running it run under pressure. So look for leaks with a pressure on it. I have seen o rings seal just great when under a vacuum but leak  when pressurized. Pressure leaks are a lot easier to find than vacuum leaks.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||theogre||07-07-2001||06:34 PM||||It is a federal crime in the US for unlicensed persons to purchace/use/etc R12. Fines for shops/persons selling to unlicensed individuals are so large they have put companies out of business. The EPA takes this Very seriously. Last I heard, there is a substantial reward for turning in shops selling R12 and other banned refrigerants.<P>R134 is proven to work just fine in Fiero systems and is currently legal for anyone to handle.<P>It is NOT simple to properly convert systems to R134. It's easier with fixed displacement compressors like the A6 family. V5 family systems are much more complicated. (V5 is used in 87-88 4 cyl Fiero.)<P>My existing cave article will probably be updated in the next couple weeks to better cover the V5 systems. Existing rules for A6 probably will not change much.||||reg||1
Z||000018||cadaver||07-08-2001||11:31 AM||||Ok and to get an air conditioning license it was all of $25 and 1/2 an hour.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Doni Hagan||07-08-2001||08:15 PM||||Okay, here's the deal....<P>After a visit to the Cave, I had a local A/C shop evacuate the system, got a good deal on a new accumulator, inspected and-or replaced the o-rings/seals/oriface tube and filled the system with 134a. <P>It works fine. I only use the AC when my wife is with me but she hasn't complained yet. She drives a Seville STS and you can almost see your breath in that thing. I tell her we should store our meat in her car. <P>Thanks, fellow members, for your help. ||||reg||6
